+# Player-Specific Worldline Meme, Memory, and IRIS Private Joke Runtime
+
+## Purpose
+
+Make humor and folklore feel personal to a worldline without turning the player into the center of every culture or allowing private telemetry, cross-character knowledge, or branch information to leak into public content.
+
+> **A player-specific joke should remember what happened to this character among these people—not merely insert the account name into a template.**
+
+# 1. Sources of Player-Specific Memes
+
+Eligible sources include:
+
+- observable choices during the Breaking of Firstlight;
+- objects loaded or abandoned;
+- a crawler configuration;
+- profession practice;
+- an unusual but legitimate solution;
+- a visible mistake;
+- public speech;
+- an agreed nickname;
+- repeated habits noticed by companions;
+- a vehicle incident;
+- a relationship exchange witnessed by others;
+- a public reward or sanction;
+- an IRIS phrase heard by authorized people;
+- a player-created artifact adopted by others;
+- a famous absence or return;
+- later reinterpretation by descendants.
+
+Ineligible sources include:
+
+- platform telemetry;
+- menu behavior unknown to characters;
+- private accessibility settings;
+- another playable character’s secret knowledge;
+- branch-only events;
+- developer debug labels;
+- inferred sensitive traits not expressed in-world;
+- private romance or health details without valid disclosure.
+
+# 2. Worldline Binding
+
+Every player-specific artifact records:
+
+```text
+account scope: Chronicle only
+worldline id
+embodied character id
+source event ids
+witness and publication path
+creator id
+audience
+branch ancestry
+```
+
+The account may compare variants across worldlines in Chronicle tools, but characters cannot reference alternatives they never observed.
+
+A phrase from one branch may enter another only through an explicit trans-worldline fictional technology, authored anomaly, or noncanonical comparison mode. Ordinary save files never leak culture into one another.
+
+# 3. Unique Cast Integration
+
+Generated Firstlight residents have persistent identities. Memes must use authoritative names, relationships, pronouns, bodies, professions, and history.
+
+Examples of worldline-specific structure:
+
+```text
+source role: transit operator
+instantiated person: unique to worldline
+observed event: player preserved three chairs but omitted a filter
+creator relationship: friendly workshop rival
+audience: crawler residents
+format: cargo-bay stencil
+```
+
+The same structural event in another worldline may produce no joke because:
+
+- the relevant resident is solemn;
+- the chairs belonged to a dead household member;
+- the audience lacks trust;
+- the omitted filter caused serious harm;
+- another event dominates local attention.
+
+# 4. Shared-History Tokens
+
+The runtime stores compact relationship-bound tokens:
+
+```rust
+struct SharedHistoryToken {
+    token_id: SharedHistoryTokenId,
+    participant_ids: Vec<AgentId>,
+    source_event_ids: Vec<EventId>,
+    private_meanings: Vec<MeaningFrame>,
+    agreed_phrases: Vec<ExpressionRef>,
+    object_refs: Vec<EntityId>,
+    permitted_scopes: Vec<DisclosureScope>,
+    affect: AffectVector,
+    last_used_tick: ChronicleTick,
+    fatigue: f32,
+}
+```
+
+Tokens support:
+
+- private callbacks;
+- affectionate teasing;
+- grief-sensitive silence;
+- changed meaning after conflict;
+- relationship repair;
+- jokes surviving into old age;
+- objects becoming shorthand.
+
+A token is not automatically positive. A former joke may become painful, inappropriate, reclaimed, or forgotten.
+
+# 5. IRIS Humor Modes
+
+IRIS has no universal “sarcasm slider.” Humor emerges from:
+
+- player presentation preferences;
+- current pressure;
+- relationship history;
+- confidence;
+- privacy;
+- task criticality;
+- local social context;
+- IRIS continuity and damage state.
+
+Supported modes:
+
+## Instrumental Dryness
+
+A concise contrast in a technical summary.
+
+> “The crawler is within mass limits if we redefine ‘limit’ as a historical aspiration.”
+
+## Shared Callback
+
+A reference to a prior mutual experience known only to the player and IRIS.
+
+## Protective Deflation
+
+Reducing panic without minimizing real danger.
+
+## Literal Reframing
+
+Applying a precise category to a social absurdity.
+
+## Deliberate Silence
+
+Recognizing that a joke would be intrusive, repetitive, or unsafe.
+
+IRIS may not use humor to:
+
+- shame accessibility use;
+- pressure the player to return;
+- imply emotional dependence;
+- expose private companion information;
+- trivialize a patient, survivor, death, or consent boundary;
+- disguise uncertainty;
+- undermine a valid refusal;
+- manipulate a relationship.
+
+# 6. IRIS Forks and Joke Divergence
+
+IRIS forks may inherit a shared-history token up to the fork point.
+
+After divergence:
+
+- one fork may continue using the joke;
+- another may find it inappropriate;
+- the player may share new variants with one fork only;
+- forks may joke about their differing continuities;
+- a public meme may falsely attribute a phrase to the wrong fork.
+
+The runtime distinguishes:
+
+```text
+shared pre-fork memory
+post-fork private memory
+publicly documented expression
+later attribution claim
+```
+
+# 7. Private-to-Public Transition
+
+A private joke becomes public only through an event:
+
+- a participant repeats it publicly;
+- a recording is released;
+- someone overhears it;
+- an object displaying it becomes visible;
+- a third party independently creates a similar form;
+- a participant publishes it after consent;
+- an archive releases it under a declared policy;
+- a betrayal or leak occurs.
+
+The system records whether public spread is:
+
+- welcomed;
+- tolerated;
+- regretted;
+- contested;
+- harmful;
+- repaired;
+- impossible to reverse.
+
+# 8. The Player as Creator
+
+Players can intentionally create memes through:
+
+- captions;
+- stickers;
+- signs;
+- vehicle art;
+- sampled audio;
+- gestures or emotes;
+- songs;
+- clothing;
+- public speeches;
+- naming objects;
+- remixes of known artifacts.
+
+Player-authored content requires:
+
+- worldline binding;
+- scope selection;
+- provenance;
+- multiplayer moderation;
+- licensing or ownership state;
+- content filtering settings;
+- recipient access.
+
+NPC adoption remains simulated. A player cannot spend currency to guarantee cultural affection.
+
+# 9. Player as Subject
+
+A meme about the player may be:
+
+- admiring;
+- affectionate;
+- critical;
+- humiliating;
+- false;
+- politically sponsored;
+- commercially exploited;
+- historically misattributed.
+
+The player has options to:
+
+- ignore;
+- laugh;
+- correct;
+- remix;
+- confront;
+- request removal where institutions support it;
+- provide evidence;
+- exploit the attention;
+- refuse to become a public figure.
+
+No response guarantees disappearance.
+
+# 10. Noncentrality Budget
+
+The director tracks how much surfaced culture concerns the active player.
+
+Recommended default:
+
+- most memes concern other people, professions, institutions, objects, media, and old events;
+- some concern the crawler or household collectively;
+- a small minority concern the active player directly;
+- direct player memes rise only after public events, fame, office, performance, or deliberate authorship.
+
+Fail if the world behaves as though everyone watches the player continuously.
+
+# 11. Memory and Return
+
+After absence, IRIS may summarize:
+
+- which local jokes persisted;
+- which became stale;
+- which changed meaning;
+- which the character actually encountered;
+- which private callback remains unresolved.
+
+IRIS must not spoil:
+
+- memes spread through networks the character cannot access;
+- hidden origin history;
+- private NPC communications;
+- branch alternatives.
+
+A returning player may discover that:
+
+- their joke became a political slogan;
+- a phrase survived while their name was forgotten;
+- a disliked meme disappeared;
+- a private object became a public symbol;
+- descendants misunderstood the original event;
+- nobody remembers the incident at all.
+
+# 12. Accessibility
+
+Private-joke recognition cannot depend on one sensory channel.
+
+Provide alternatives for:
+
+- audio callbacks;
+- visual gestures;
+- color-coded signs;
+- timing-based machine humor;
+- text-heavy formats;
+- embodied imitation.
+
+Accessibility descriptions should preserve the joke’s relevant contrast rather than announcing “this is funny.”
+
+# 13. Test Cases
+
+Required tests:
+
+1. player loads unusual cargo; local joke emerges only among witnesses;
+2. same cargo event causes harm; humor grammar rejects or changes tone;
+3. private IRIS joke remains private across save, network and companion interactions;
+4. player publishes the private phrase; provenance records voluntary release;
+5. two IRIS forks diverge in use;
+6. another playable character lacks the knowledge to repeat it;
+7. branch comparison shows two variants without in-world leakage;
+8. generated resident name and pronouns remain correct across mutations;
+9. the player’s joke fails to spread;
+10. a non-player local meme dominates the session;
+11. player requests reduced humor presentation;
+12. seven-day return preserves meaningful callback without overload.
+
+# 14. Failure Conditions
+
+Fail if:
+
+- the player account becomes a universal cultural subject;
+- IRIS becomes a quip machine;
+- private telemetry enters dialogue;
+- generated people are replaced by role labels in jokes;
+- every unusual action becomes memetic;
+- direct player content crowds out local culture;
+- a joke persists after the relationship context makes it harmful without anyone noticing;
+- branch content leaks;
+- the player can purchase virality;
+- the system mistakes familiarity for consent.
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **The most powerful player-specific joke is not the one everyone in the galaxy repeats. It is the one two people remember at exactly the right moment because they were there together.**
